<ModellingLayout title="Measurements, amounts and values" description="Two numbers may disagree because they answer different questions. Identify the subject, unit and stated basis before deciding whether values can be compared." proseClass="prose wide modelling-document learning-document">
  <h2 id="two-areas">68 m² or 72 m²: which is right?</h2>
  <p>Two accounts give different floor areas for Flat 1 at fictional Harbour Court. One says 68 m²; the other says 72 m². Before choosing the newer account, averaging the values or labelling one an error, look at what each includes.</p>
  <LearningIllustration scene="surveying" alt="A surveying desk with aligned floor-plan overlays, a measuring tool and an enclosed-area detail." />
  <p>For this example, the same dwelling consists of a main area of 68 m² and an adjoining enclosed area of exactly 4 m². The areas do not overlap. There has been no physical change between the two accounts; they use the same source dimensions and differ only in the inclusion convention stated below. These are complete fictional premises, not an industry measuring standard. They do not imply that the enclosed area was physically accessed during the story's inspection.</p>
  <div class="learning-spread">
    <section class="learning-specimen" aria-labelledby="account-a">
      <h3 id="account-a">Account A: main area only</h3>
      <dl><dt>Subject</dt><dd>Flat 1 at Harbour Court.</dd><dt>Value and unit</dt><dd>68 m².</dd><dt>Stated basis</dt><dd>Main area; excludes the enclosed 4 m² area.</dd><dt>Source</dt><dd>Fictional area account A, using the supplied plan dimensions.</dd></dl>
    </section>
    <section class="learning-specimen" aria-labelledby="account-b">
      <h3 id="account-b">Account B: main and enclosed areas</h3>
      <dl><dt>Subject</dt><dd>The same Flat 1 at Harbour Court.</dd><dt>Value and unit</dt><dd>72 m².</dd><dt>Stated basis</dt><dd>Main area plus the enclosed 4 m² area.</dd><dt>Source</dt><dd>Fictional area account B, using the same supplied plan dimensions.</dd></dl>
    </section>
  </div>
  <LearningDiagram kind="measurement" caption="Schematic, not to scale: the same main area is 68 m² in both accounts. A excludes the separate enclosed 4 m² area; B includes it, producing 72 m²." />
  <p>The arithmetic is simple once the meaning is visible: 68 + 4 = 72. The stated inclusion difference fully explains these values under the supplied premises. Both accounts can be accurate statements of their own quantities. We have not shown that either basis is appropriate for a particular professional, contractual or legal use.</p>
  <p>This is why “the area” is an incomplete description. It leaves someone else to choose the subject and inclusion boundary, perhaps without noticing. Preserve the basis beside the quantity so a reader can decide whether it answers their question.</p>

  <h2 id="quantity-account">What a useful quantity account preserves</h2>
  <p>The minimum intelligible statement identifies what is being quantified, the value, unit and relevant basis. “72” has no unit; “72 m²” still has no subject or inclusion convention. Attaching it to a building record does not establish whether it measures the building, Flat 1, a shared part or a plot.</p>
  <p>Other qualifications become important for particular questions. Method explains how the value was obtained: for example, direct observation or a calculation from source dimensions. Source identifies the account or evidence. Relevant time tells us which condition of the subject was measured. Precision and rounding describe how finely a value is stated; more decimal places do not by themselves mean better evidence.</p>
  <p>These distinctions let a reviewer ask focused questions without choosing a formal representation. They also prevent an exchange from retaining the number while losing the reason it differs. A copied “72 m²” is not meaning-preserving if its recipient assumes that the enclosed area was excluded.</p>

  <section class="learning-practice" aria-labelledby="area-practice">
    <h2 id="area-practice">Practice: explain the difference, then change the case</h2>
    <p>A colleague writes, “Account B corrects Account A by adding the missing 4 m².” Is that warranted?</p>
    <h3>Worked response</h3>
    <p>No. The premises say A deliberately excludes that area. B uses a different inclusion convention; it is not described as a correction. A better note is: “Both concern Flat 1. A reports 68 m² excluding the enclosed 4 m² area; B reports 72 m² including it. Their 4 m² difference is explained by that stated basis.”</p>
    <h3>A different pair of accounts</h3>
    <p>Now consider Cedar Flat. Accounts C and D give 68 m² and 72 m², and both say “main and enclosed areas included”. Their measurement methods, source dimensions and relevant dates are absent. No relationship to Harbour Court's dimensions is stipulated.</p>
    <p>The previous explanation no longer settles the difference. Ask for the actual subject extent, methods, dimensions, dates and rounding conventions. The records appear to disagree on their stated basis, but we cannot yet conclude that they are incompatible measurements of the same unchanged condition using comparable methods. Nor can we explain the gap as an excluded 4 m² area when both say it is included.</p>
    <p>A useful request is: “Please supply the measurement date and method for accounts C and D, and clarify their inclusion boundaries. We need to know whether these are comparable accounts before describing one as a correction.” That asks for evidence, not an arbitrary winning number.</p>
  </section>

  <h2 id="amounts">A common currency does not make amounts interchangeable</h2>
  <p>The same problem appears in amounts. Add three invented statements about the sale of Flat 1 in Harbour Court transaction T. All concern that dwelling and use pounds sterling. Each still answers a different question.</p>
  <div class="learning-table">
    <table>
      <caption>Invented amount statements; no pricing or valuation advice</caption>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Statement</th><th scope="col">Maker and purpose</th><th scope="col">What the date concerns</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">Asking price: £300,000</th><td>The seller's marketing instruction to the agent: the amount being asked for the dwelling.</td><td>The instruction says this asking amount applies from 1 September 2026.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Agreed transaction price: £292,000</th><td>The buyer and seller's fictional transaction record states the amount they agreed in transaction T.</td><td>The recorded agreement occurred on 9 September 2026; no further legal status is implied.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Valuation opinion: £295,000</th><td>A fictional valuer's opinion for the stated purpose “assessment under valuation instruction V”, under assumptions recorded in that opinion.</td><td>The opinion is expressed as at 5 September 2026 and issued on 7 September.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>The asking-price statement describes a marketing position. The agreed-price statement concerns a particular transaction. The valuation is an attributed opinion for a purpose and date. Their different values do not, by themselves, establish a conflict. Even if all three amounts were £295,000, they would remain different claims.</p>
  <p>Purpose matters because a statement may be suitable for one question and insufficient for another. Nothing here permits us to replace a valuation opinion with the asking price or determine what a lender should accept. The model makes the difference reviewable; it does not deliver the professional judgement.</p>
  <section class="learning-practice" aria-labelledby="amount-practice">
    <h2 id="amount-practice">Practice: challenge a misleading summary</h2>
    <p>A summary selects the 9 September statement because it has the latest date and labels £292,000 “latest valuation”. What has been lost?</p>
    <h3>Worked response</h3>
    <p>The amount's meaning, maker and purpose have changed. The latest supplied agreement is not a valuation opinion. Preserve “agreed transaction price in T” and its agreement date; keep the 5 September valuation opinion separate with its issue date and stated purpose. Choosing a latest date cannot turn one type of claim into another.</p>
  </section>
  <p class="learning-note">A review can often begin with one sentence: “Please retain what this value measures or asserts, not just its number and unit.” For dates and periods themselves, see <a href="/semantic-modelling/explore/dates-periods-and-applicability">Dates, periods and applicability</a>; for deciding what a source supports, see <a href="/semantic-modelling/explore/claims-evidence-and-uncertainty">Claims, evidence and uncertainty</a>.</p>
</ModellingLayout>
